**The most common fetch mistake: forgetting `res.ok`.**

This code looks correct but swallows every server error silently:

```ts
const res = await fetch(url);
const data = await res.json(); // runs even on 404 or 500!
```

Always guard it:

```ts
const res = await fetch(url);
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
const data = await res.json();
```

**Use `as` to tell TypeScript the shape of JSON at the network boundary.**

`res.json()` returns `Promise<any>` because TypeScript cannot inspect a live HTTP response. You need to assert the type yourself at this one point — and only at this point:

```ts
interface Meal {
  idMeal: string;
  strMeal: string;
  strMealThumb: string;
  strCategory: string;
}

type MealSearchResponse = { meals: Meal[] | null };

const data = await res.json() as MealSearchResponse;
```

`as MealSearchResponse` is a **type assertion** — you are telling TypeScript "trust me, this JSON has this shape". Use `as` *only* at JSON/network boundaries where TypeScript genuinely cannot infer the shape. Once you've asserted the type here, it flows automatically through the rest of your component — so you won't need another `as` for this data. (You'll still meet `as` occasionally elsewhere, like narrowing a union or casting a DOM element — just don't reach for it to silence ordinary type errors.)
